WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:06.610
In January 2021, the Tolkien fan-site TheOneRing.net
leaked the synopsis for Amazon's upcoming

00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:07.960
Lord of the Rings series.

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:09.950
Soon after, Amazon confirmed it was real.

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:12.519
But what does it tell us about the show and
its setting?

00:00:12.519 --> 00:00:15.000
Here's what we learned from the Lord of the
Rings synopsis leak.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.599
It's been pretty obvious for a while now that
the new series will be set during Middle-Earth's

00:00:19.599 --> 00:00:20.599
Second Age.

00:00:20.599 --> 00:00:25.609
In March 2019, Amazon Studios released a series
of increasingly detailed maps on the show's

00:00:25.609 --> 00:00:29.470
Twitter page, culminating with a final image
that clearly showed Middle-earth during the

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:31.269
Second Age of Tolkien's writings.

00:00:31.269 --> 00:00:32.599
The accompanying tweet read:

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:34.320
"Welcome to the Second Age."

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:37.880
This got most Tolkien fans pretty excited,
and with good reason, too.

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:42.770
Despite being filled with great stories, the
Second Age is a largely under-explored area

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:43.770
of Tolkien's legendarium.

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:48.230
Because that era spans some 3,500 years, however,
it was still pretty tough to nail down just

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:51.710
when exactly during the Second Age the show
is going to be set.

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:53.500
Now, however, things are a little clearer.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:57.250
Along with reconfirming the Second Age setting,
the synopsis also mentions that the show will

00:00:57.250 --> 00:01:00.030
begin, quote, "in a time of relative peace."

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:03.900
This is important, as the latter half of the
Second Age, in particular, is anything but

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:04.900
peaceful.

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:07.881
Chances are, this means that the show is due
to begin sometime between the fall of the

00:01:07.881 --> 00:01:11.659
original Dark Lord Morgoth and the re-emergence
of his chief lieutenant, Sauron.

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:16.420
One thing the leaked synopsis mentions is
that the show will "take viewers back to an

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:18.590
era in which great powers were forged."

00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:19.590
Sound familiar?

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:23.159
Yes, there's a good chance Amazon is here
referring to the forging of the One Ring — as

00:01:23.159 --> 00:01:26.990
well as the other Rings of Power, which are
crucial to the tale of the Second Age.

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:30.960
All of the major Rings of Power were forged
roughly halfway through the Second Age.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:35.150
This includes three for the Elves, seven for
the Dwarves, nine for the great Lords of Men,

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:36.649
and one for the Dark Lord himself.

00:01:36.649 --> 00:01:43.400
"One Ring to bring them all, and in the darkness
bind them."

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:47.060
The Rings are originally forged and owned
solely by the Elves, who create them under

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:48.759
the guidance of a disguised Sauron.

00:01:48.759 --> 00:01:52.710
Later, when war breaks out between the Elves
and Sauron, the Dark Lord gathers all but

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:56.469
three of them up and redistributes them to
Middle-Earth's more easily-conquered races.

00:01:56.469 --> 00:02:00.159
While it's almost certain that the Rings are
the "great powers" mentioned here, it should

00:02:00.159 --> 00:02:05.130
also be pointed out that the Amazon synopsis
could be talking about something else: specifically,

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:06.409
Middle-Earth's great alliances.

00:02:06.409 --> 00:02:10.220
The Third Age is largely defined by the Free
Peoples of Middle-earth fighting Sauron in

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:14.860
isolated pockets, but the Second Age actually
features several major unions — particularly

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:17.420
between the races of Elves, Men, and Dwarves.

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:21.980
The age even ends with the famous "Last Alliance,"
which takes the battle to Mordor and defeats

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:23.860
Sauron during the Siege of Barad-dûr.

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:28.430
One phrase from the synopsis that jumps out
is the suggestion that, during the Second

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:29.660
Age, "kingdoms rose to glory."

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:33.490
While there are many strange new places that
the Amazon show will doubtless spend time

00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:37.660
exploring, several of these rising kingdoms
will be quite familiar — or familiar in

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:38.660
name, at least.

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:42.940
During this period, Galadriel visits her future
kingdom in Lorien for the first time, while

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:47.380
Legolas' grandfather, Oropher, establishes
a refugee Elven Kingdom in the heart of the

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:48.380
Greenwood.

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:51.750
The Dwarven kingdom of Khazad-dûm is already
ancient when the Second Age begins, and it

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:54.470
plays a vital role in the politics of western
Middle-Earth.

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:58.600
Gondor is founded toward the end of the Second
Age too, and Elrond establishes Rivendell

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:02.420
around halfway through, in direct defiance
of an all-powerful Sauron.

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:04.200
And as for the Dark Lord himself?

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:08.159
In need of a home after his return to Middle-Earth,
he carves out a corner of the map for himself

00:03:08.159 --> 00:03:10.970
— in the dark and terrible land known as
Mordor.

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:15.850
Amazon's synopsis mentions that the Second
Age is a time in which "unlikely heroes were

00:03:15.850 --> 00:03:16.850
tested."

00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:20.540
This is a vague yet tantalizing glimpse into
the show's cast of characters, which, for

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:22.690
now, remains largely shrouded in mystery.

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:24.800
Sure, a few hints have come out here and there.

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:28.430
For instance, Ian McKellen has said he's up
for reprising his role as Gandalf, even though

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:31.470
the character doesn't arrive in Middle-Earth
until the Third Age.

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:35.890
On the flip side, Elrond actor Hugo Weaving
has made it abundantly clear he has no plans

00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:37.270
to come back to Tolkien's world.

00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:40.620
And although many of the show's cast members
have already been announced, for the most

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:44.430
part, we don't yet know for sure who's playing
who, or if the characters they're playing

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:47.960
have any relation to heroes and villains we've
already seen on screen before.

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:51.760
The most solid actor-to-character connection
that has leaked so far is that Welsh actress

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:54.460
Morfydd Clark will be playing a younger Galadriel.

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:58.280
Other casting leaks have only added to the
confusion by throwing around brand new character

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:01.490
names with no obvious relation to established
Tolkien texts.

00:04:01.490 --> 00:04:06.060
According to an October 2019 report by Deadline,
English actor Joseph Mawle will be playing

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:09.630
a character named Oren, who was intriguingly
described as being the lead villain of the

00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:10.630
series.

00:04:10.630 --> 00:04:14.870
Other new characters reportedly include the
heroic Beldor, and a female lead named Tyra,

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:16.350
played by Markella Kavenagh.

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:20.690
It was initially supposed by fans that these
were fake names disguising well-known characters.

00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:24.820
However, the synopsis states that "the series
follows an ensemble cast of characters, both

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:26.320
familiar and new..."

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:29.820
So these could well be the actual names of
brand new characters that have been made up

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:30.820
for the show.

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:34.480
Unfortunately, the new synopsis doesn't offer
up any kind of definitive answer regarding

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:36.620
which heroes will be taking on the Dark Lord.

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:40.370
It does, however, suggest that the show will
be bringing in some genuinely fresh faces

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:44.660
with no obvious roots in Lord of the Rings
lore — whether the fans like it or not.

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:48.820
In The Lord of the Rings, Gandalf implies
that Sauron's potential return wouldn't be

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:51.740
the first time Middle-Earth has been dominated
by the Dark Lord.

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:56.720
"Sauron needs only this Ring to cover all
the lands in a second darkness."

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:00.590
So if that's the second darkness that he's
talking about, when exactly was the first?

00:05:00.590 --> 00:05:02.720
Why, in the Second Age, of course.

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:05.020
The Amazon synopsis mentions that, at this
time,

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:08.690
"...hope hung by the finest of threads, and
the greatest villain that ever flowed from

00:05:08.690 --> 00:05:11.930
Tolkien's pen threatened to cover all the
world in darkness."

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:16.310
These words suggest that the Amazon series
will portray Sauron's initial rise to power,

00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:19.370
including his greatest victories and near-total
conquest of all of Middle-Earth.

00:05:19.370 --> 00:05:23.061
Of course, this doesn't just give Amazon the
opportunity to put the show's heroes through

00:05:23.061 --> 00:05:24.061
the ringer.

00:05:24.061 --> 00:05:27.540
It also allows the studio to divest him of
the one-dimensional persona that defines him

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:28.960
throughout The Lord of the Rings.

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:33.370
Instead, the show is likely to introduce fans
to a much more interesting version of Tolkien's

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:35.139
iconic villain.

00:05:36.139 --> 00:05:39.590
Sauron's key role in the synopsis suggests
what fans have believed for a long time — that

00:05:39.590 --> 00:05:41.970
the show will depict the Dark Lord in his
fairer form.

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:46.200
While the Sauron of The Lord of the Rings
is a hideous, monstrous figure — or a giant

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:50.160
eye, if you're watching the movies — he
was once able to make himself appear, well,

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:51.250
pretty darn sexy.

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:55.800
More specifically, Sauron spends much of the
Second Age masquerading as Annatar, the Lord

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:56.800
of Gifts.

00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:00.800
This charismatic disguise allows the Dark
Lord to mingle with the Elves and, for a while,

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:01.810
gain their trust.

00:06:01.810 --> 00:06:06.060
Sauron remains in his fair form for much of
this time, and only loses the ability to look

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:09.520
like this when Númenor is destroyed towards
the end of the Second Age.

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:13.810
Obviously, Sauron's prettier persona doesn't
make him trustworthy by any means — as the

00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:15.010
Elves soon discover for themselves.

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:19.580
Nonetheless, the chance to portray the Dark
Lord in this way will likely add another fascinating

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:22.720
layer to a villain not often associated with
nuance or depth.

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:26.990
There's no question that Sauron is the biggest
threat to Middle-Earth during the Second and

00:06:26.990 --> 00:06:30.850
Third Ages — but that doesn't mean he's
the only monster hiding in the shadows.

00:06:30.850 --> 00:06:34.740
Notably, the final sentence of the leaked
synopsis begins with the line "From the darkest

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:36.699
depths of the Misty Mountains..."

00:06:36.699 --> 00:06:40.180
This line has some fascinating implications
for the direction the show might take.

00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:44.050
It suggests that viewers will be taken deeper
than the thriving halls of Khazad-dûm, to

00:06:44.050 --> 00:06:47.480
explore the terrible, unexplored spaces beneath
the dwarven kingdom.

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:48.910
So what's actually down there?

00:06:48.910 --> 00:06:51.960
Well, for one, there's the Balrog known as
Durin's Bane.

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:58.340
"You know what they awoke in the darkness
of Khazad-dûm.

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:01.400
Shadow and flame."

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:06.050
This fiery demon isn't just a terror to all
and sundry; it's also fiercely independent,

00:07:06.050 --> 00:07:09.460
having found itself without a master after
the destruction of Morgoth.

00:07:09.460 --> 00:07:13.120
As such, although Durin's Bane is supposed
to be asleep for much of the Second Age, it

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:15.630
could still turn out to be an important part
of the Amazon series.

00:07:15.630 --> 00:07:19.070
But the Balrog isn't the only thing living
beneath the Misty Mountains.

00:07:19.070 --> 00:07:23.389
When Gandalf discusses his battle under Moria
in The Two Towers, he mentions that:

00:07:23.389 --> 00:07:28.270
"Far, far below the deepest delvings of the
Dwarves, the world is gnawed by nameless things.

00:07:28.270 --> 00:07:29.860
Even Sauron knows them not.

00:07:29.860 --> 00:07:31.050
They are older than he."

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:34.710
Considering Sauron and the Balrogs fought
together in the First Age, it's clear that

00:07:34.710 --> 00:07:38.699
Gandalf is referring to something altogether
more horrifying than Durin's Bane.

00:07:38.699 --> 00:07:42.671
And while Tolkien never elaborated on what
creatures lurk in those darkest depths, it

00:07:42.671 --> 00:07:45.890
seems that the show may just tackle that question
for itself.

00:07:46.890 --> 00:07:50.639
One of the more specific lines in the synopsis
is the brief mention that the story will span

00:07:50.639 --> 00:07:53.100
"to the majestic forests of the elf-capital
of Lindon."

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:56.509
This naturally leads to the question: what
and where is Lindon?

00:07:56.509 --> 00:08:00.850
The short answer is that Lindon is an important
Elven nation that exists throughout the Second

00:08:00.850 --> 00:08:01.850
Age.

00:08:01.850 --> 00:08:05.550
More than that, though, Lindon is a very complex
and important place that plays a vital role

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:07.520
in many of Middle-Earth's stories.

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:11.610
It sits in the western reaches of the continent,
right next to the area that eventually becomes

00:08:11.610 --> 00:08:12.729
the Shire.

00:08:12.729 --> 00:08:16.070
It's also the place where the Grey Havens
are located, which is where Frodo and Bilbo

00:08:16.070 --> 00:08:19.170
sail from, into the West, at the end of Return
of the King.

00:08:19.170 --> 00:08:22.150
In the Second Age, Lindon is one of the most
powerful kingdoms in Middle-Earth.

00:08:22.150 --> 00:08:26.889
As Sauron's darkness spreads across the world,
it becomes a beacon of hope to the Free Peoples

00:08:26.889 --> 00:08:28.610
who stand against the Dark Lord.

00:08:28.610 --> 00:08:33.580
Ruled by the Elven King Gil-galad, Lindon
also plays a key role in forging crucial alliances

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:37.729
with Men and other Elves, and ultimately provides
much of the muscle needed to topple Sauron

00:08:37.729 --> 00:08:39.200
at the end of the age.

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:43.789
The last sentence of the synopsis states that
the show will also take viewers "to the breathtaking

00:08:43.789 --> 00:08:45.420
island kingdom of Númenor."

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:49.450
The involvement of this island nation of Men
was a forgone conclusion from the first moment

00:08:49.450 --> 00:08:51.010
the Second Age was confirmed.

00:08:51.010 --> 00:08:54.690
Nevertheless, knowing that Númenor will get
its fair share of the spotlight is exciting

00:08:54.690 --> 00:08:57.070
news for fans of Tolkien's legendarium.

00:08:57.070 --> 00:09:00.950
The godlike beings known as the Valar gift
the island to its inhabitants early in the

00:09:00.950 --> 00:09:04.520
Second Age, and the Men of Númenor continue
to flourish there for centuries.

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:07.960
The Men of Númenor eventually get tangled
up with events on the mainland and, for a

00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:10.670
while, become the focus of Sauron's obsession.

00:09:10.670 --> 00:09:14.690
Unfortunately, the Dark Lord's machinations
ultimately lead to the total destruction of

00:09:14.690 --> 00:09:16.790
the island and the near-extinction of its
people.

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:21.350
One of the final lines in the synopsis reveals
that the show's characters will go "to the

00:09:21.350 --> 00:09:22.950
furthest reaches of the map."

00:09:22.950 --> 00:09:26.790
This is a particularly interesting statement,
as it can basically mean one of two things.

00:09:26.790 --> 00:09:30.770
On one hand, this could be a veiled reference
to the Blessed Realm of Aman, the land that

00:09:30.770 --> 00:09:32.360
lies hidden in the West.

00:09:32.360 --> 00:09:36.460
This is where the Valar and the Elves live
in blissful harmony, and it's also where Middle-Earth's

00:09:36.460 --> 00:09:39.490
Elves sail after their time on the continent
comes to an end.

00:09:39.490 --> 00:09:43.620
Otherwise known as the Undying Lands, this
continent and its blessed dwellings are focal

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:44.950
points of the First Age of Middle-Earth.

00:09:44.950 --> 00:09:49.270
However, Valinor also plays a role in the
events of the Second Age; most notably with

00:09:49.270 --> 00:09:51.760
regards to the eventual downfall of Númenor.

00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:55.600
On the other hand, the synopsis could be referring
to the eastern regions of Middle-earth.

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:59.460
These are areas of the map that were hardly
ever explored, even by Tolkien himself.

00:09:59.460 --> 00:10:04.100
However, Sauron's influence is particularly
strong in these lands, and the Dark Lord recruits

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:05.830
many of his soldiers from their inhabitants.

00:10:05.830 --> 00:10:10.130
If the Amazon show does head in this direction,
it's likely that Tolkien fans will get to

00:10:10.130 --> 00:10:13.459
experience a side of Middle-Earth they've
never seen before.

00:10:13.459 --> 00:10:15.760
